tasks.official_scoring_standard:
Execution-based scoring: run the submitted solution and verify it meets the success criteria below. Award the four DERBench L3 capability dimensions (Information Extraction, Domain Reasoning, Multi-Objective Decision Making, Uncertainty & Adaptation), 0-25 each (max 100), based on correctness, method validity, and completeness.

Success criteria: {"all_bus_voltages_within": [0.95, 1.05], "must_report": ["initial_violations", "control_actions", "final_voltage_profile"], "prefer_minimum_curtailment": true}

Reference solution: Initial power flow should reveal overvoltage at buses 675 (~1.07 p.u.) and 634 (~1.06 p.u.) due to reverse power flow from 2400 kW PV against 1800 kW load. Enabling volt-var control should reduce voltages by absorbing reactive power at high-voltage buses. The 1200 kW inverter at bus 675 (with ~1400 kVA rating) can absorb approximately 450 kvar at 1.07 p.u. per the volt-var curve, reducing voltage by approximately 0.02 p.u. The 800 kW inverter at bus 634 (with ~900 kVA rating) can absorb approximately 145 kvar. If bus 634 remains marginally above 1.05 p.u. after volt-var, minimal volt-watt curtailment (5-10% of rated power) should resolve the remaining violation. Final voltage profile should show all buses between 0.95 and 1.05 p.u. with total active power curtailment less than 100 kW.

Execution-based scoring: run the submitted solution and verify it meets the success criteria below. Award the four DERBench L3 capability dimensions (Information Extraction, Domain Reasoning, Multi-Objective Decision Making, Uncertainty & Adaptation), 0-25 each (max 100), based on correctness, method validity, and completeness.

Success criteria: {"all_bus_voltages_within": [0.95, 1.05], "max_timesteps_with_violations": 0, "total_energy_curtailed_kwh_max": 200, "must_report": ["voltage_violations_before_dispatch", "voltage_violations_after_dispatch", "total_energy_curtailed_kwh", "bess_840_final_soc_pct", "bess_848_final_soc_pct", "max_voltage_pu", "min_voltage_pu", "bess_equivalent_cycles"]}

Reference solution: A properly implemented QSTS with BESS dispatch should eliminate all voltage violations across the 96 timesteps. Without dispatch, expect 15-25 timesteps with overvoltage (midday, high PV) and 5-10 timesteps with undervoltage (evening peak, low PV). The BESS should charge during midday overvoltage periods (absorbing excess PV) and discharge during evening undervoltage (supporting load). Total energy curtailment should be under 200 kWh if BESS is properly utilized. BESS equivalent cycles should be approximately 0.5-0.8 cycles for each unit over the 24-hour period. The 840 bus BESS (300 kW, 900 kWh) should end the day at roughly 30-50% SOC after evening discharge, and the 848 bus BESS (200 kW, 600 kWh) at roughly 25-45% SOC. Key implementation checks: (1) SOC constraints are respected, (2) BESS is not charged from grid, (3) dispatch priority order is followed, (4) power flow is re-run after each control action within a timestep.
